<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dharwad</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dharwad is a city in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, forming a twin city with Hubballi. Its fort was held by the Adil Shahis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a> before the British took the region in 1818. Seat of Karnatak University, founded in 1949, it is known as a centre of education and of Hindustani classical music, and it lent its name to the famous Dharwad pedha sweet.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dharwad.jpg" alt="Dharwad" width="320" height="164" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kalpavruksha3363 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:IIIT_Dharwad.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Dharwad, formerly Dharwar, is a city in the northwestern part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a> and the headquarters of Dharwad district. It lies 430 km northwest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengaluru">Bangalore</a> on NH-48, between Bangalore and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pune">Pune</a>, at 750 metres above sea level. It forms a contiguous urban area with Hubballi and merged with it in 1962 to form the twin cities of Hubballi-Dharwad. The name is derived from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> dwarawata, from dwara, door, and wata or wada, town. For centuries the town was a resting place for travellers and a gateway between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malnad">Malenadu</a>, the western mountains, and the Bayalu Seeme, the plains.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukyas</a> ruled Dharwad in the 12th century, and an inscription at the Durgadevi temple names a ruler, Bhaskara Deva, in 1117. In the 14th century the district was overrun by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate</a> and then annexed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> kingdom of Vijayanagar. According to local tradition an official named Dharav built the fort in 1403.</p><p>After the defeat of Vijayanagar at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-talikota">Battle of Talikota</a> in 1565 Dharwad was for a few years practically independent under its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> governor. In 1573 the sultan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adil-shah">Adil Shah</a>, captured the fort and annexed the town. His second fort raised its strategic importance and drew later conquerors.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a> took the fort in 1685. When the Mughal empire broke up, Dharwad fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha Empire</a>. Hyder Ali of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a> overran the province in 1764 and captured the fort in 1778, and the Marathas retook it in 1791. After the final defeat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshwa">Peshwa</a> by the British in 1818 the region joined the Bombay Presidency of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>. Rulers such as Baba Saheb of Nargund and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kittur-chennamma">Kittur Chennamma</a> opposed the British expansion in the early 19th century. The Dharwad Municipal Council first came into existence on 1 January 1856.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Dharwad is called the cultural capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>. Musicians from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-india">North India</a> halted there on the way to the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a>, and the region became a centre of Hindustani classical music, especially the Kirana and Jaipur-Atrauli gharanas. It is the home of the singers Gangubai Hangal and Mallikarjun Mansur, and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> writers Da Ra Bendre, V. K. Gokak and Girish Karnad, who won the Jnanpith Award. Karnatak College was founded in 1917 and Karnatak University in 1949. Dharwad peda, a sweet of milk and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a>, has been accorded a Geographical Indication. The twin cities had a population of 943,857 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 2011.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dharwad” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The territory lay between Belgaum and <b>Dharwad</b> in the fertile black-soil country of northern Karnataka.</blockquote><a href="/state/kittur-state">Kittur State</a></li><li><blockquote>The Nawabs ruled a territory of black-soil cotton and grain country between <b>Dharwad</b> and the Tungabhadra.</blockquote><a href="/state/savanur-state">Savanur State</a></li></ul></section>
